mplications\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Delavirdine functions as an inhibitor of CYP2D6 and causes reversible elevations in liver enzyme levels when used in combination with other antiretrovirals like saquinavir. This inhibition can lead to increased plasma concentrations of co-administered drugs that are substrates of these pathways, potentially raising the risk of adverse effects. Clinical guidance emphasizes the need for frequent monitoring of liver enzyme function when delavirdine is part of a combination regimen [Data: Relationships (1489, 1491); Entities (4592)]. Its role as a CYP3A4 inhibitor also positions it as a potential alternative to ritonavir for boosting protease inhibitor concentrations, though data on safety and efficacy in such use remain limited [Data: Relationships (56831)].\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"药时曲线下面积 (AUC) is a critical pharmacokinetic indicator affected by antiretroviral regimens\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The药时曲线下面积 (Area Under the Curve, AUC) serves as a key pharmacokinetic metric for assessing drug exposure over time. Within this community, AUC is directly influenced by drug interactions involving antiretrovirals. For example, delavirdine increases the AUC of protease inhibitors like indinavir and saquinavir due to its inhibitory effect on CYP3A4. Similarly, ritonavir-boosted regimens (e.g., atazanavir/ritonavir, amprenavir/ritonavir) are designed to elevate and sustain AUC levels of co-administered protease inhibitors, enhancing antiviral efficacy [Data: Entities (13886); Relationships (56831)]. Monitoring AUC is therefore essential in managing therapeutic outcomes and avoiding toxicity in HIV treatment.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Ritonavir-boosted protease inhibitor combinations are central to drug interaction risks\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Combinations such as atazanavir/ritonavir and amprenavir/ritonavir are classified under the protease inhibitor drug class and are frequently used with ritonavir as a pharmacokinetic enhancer. Ritonavir strongly inhibits CYP3A4 and drug transporters, making these boosted regimens major contributors to drug-drug interactions (DDIs). Real-world studies indicate that ritonavir- or cobicistat-boosted antiretroviral therapies account for over half of reported clinically significant DDIs in HIV patients [Data: Relationships (59407, 56831)]. These interactions necessitate careful dose adjustments or avoidance of concomitant medications, particularly with strong CYP inducers like rifampin.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Non-nucleoside reverse transcriptase inhibitors share metabolic interaction profiles\",\n            \"explanation\": \"The drug class '非核苷类似物' (non-nucleoside analogs, i.e., NNRTIs) includes efavirenz and delavirdine and is characterized by significant effects on cytochrome P450 enzymes, particularly CYP2D6. As a class, NNRTIs can induce or inhibit metabolic pathways, thereby altering the concentrations of co-administered drugs. This shared mechanism underlies many of the observed drug interactions in this community, including reduced concentrations of macrolide antibiotics (e.g., clarithromycin) and azole antifungals when used with NNRTIs [Data: Entities (12138); Relationships (4903)]. Such interactions require substitution strategies (e.g., using azithromycin instead of clarithromycin) to maintain therapeutic efficacy.\"\n        },\n        {\n            \"summary\": \"Liver enzyme monitoring is essential in regimens containing delavirdine\",\n            \"explanation\": \"Delavirdine’s use is associated with reversible elevations in liver enzyme levels, particularly when combined with other hepatotoxic agents like certain protease inhibitors. This effect necessitates regular hepatic function monitoring during treatment. The evidence notes that while delavirdine may enhance the efficacy of drugs like saquinavir, the concurrent risk of liver enzym